{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And He said, If you will surely hearken to the voice of HaShem.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat), it says: if you fulfill the Torah, you will never fall ill from any sickness — not from natural illnesses, which come from the foods that enter the body, nor from accidental illnesses, which come from outside the body. And when it says 'the voice of HaShem your Elohim,' the intent is upon the Ten Commandments, all of which were His voice, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 4:12): ״קול דברים אתם שומעים״ — for all ten commandments that we heard from His mouth were heard by the ear, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 20:1): ״וידבר אלהים את כל הדברים האלה לאמר״. And after this it mentions the three categories of commandments, which are the intelligible ones, the received ones, and the statutes.<br><b>and what is upright in His eyes you shall do,</b> these are the mishpatim, which are the intelligible commandments; and thus it is written (Nehemiah 9:13): ״ותתן להם משפטים ישרים״.<br><b>and you shall hearken to His commandments,</b> these are the received commandments (mitzvot), which no person can find through his reason and must reflect upon them and incline his ear toward them; therefore it says, והאזנת למצותיו, and it is written (Proverbs 15:31): ״אזן שמעת תוכחת חיים״.<br><b>and you shall keep all His statutes,</b> these are the statutes (ḥukim) whose reason is not revealed to all. And Scripture says: if you fulfill all this, you will be guarded from all sickness — it goes without saying that you will be guarded from every kind of the disease of Egypt which you knew and saw with your own eyes, that they were afflicted by it; and therefore it frightens them with that disease. For even from illness that comes to a person from the natural course and the customary order of the world, you will be guarded.<br><b>for I am HaShem who heals you,</b> I will guard your health so that no sickness comes upon you at all." "<b>And the Ramban wrote:</b> 'All the disease which I put upon Egypt' — it is not the way of a person to promise his servant: if you do all my will and desire, I will not kill you with evil diseases; and so too all the promises in the Torah. Rather, this is a warning, that He warns them not to be among those who rebel against Him like the Egyptians; for when they hearken to His voice they will be saved from all that disease, because that disease is fit to come upon all who transgress His will, as it came upon Egypt, who did not hearken to Him. And this is in the manner of ״והשיב בך את כל מדוה מצרים״ — and it says ״כי אני ה׳ רופאך״ as a promise that He will remove from among them a disease that comes in the manner of all the land, as He healed the waters — end of quotation." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'And He said' — this is Tiferet. 'To the voice' — this is Knesset Yisrael, and it is the Glory (Hod) concerning which we were warned (Exodus 23:22): ״כי אם שמוע תשמע בקולו״. And the meaning of the verse is: if you hearken to that voice, to keep the commandments of HaShem and His statutes — this is Tiferet — 'all the disease which I put upon Egypt' — 'I will not put upon you, for I am HaShem who heals you,' that is to say, the Glory (Hod), which is 'I,' and this is clear." "<b>And in the Sefer ha-Bahir:</b> 'And He said, If you will surely hearken to the voice of HaShem your Elohim' — this verse is interpreted with reference to the evil impulse (yetzer ha-ra), because the complaint mentioned in the preceding verse was from the side of the evil impulse. And therefore they said there: 'If you will surely hearken to the voice of HaShem your Elohim, and you shall hearken to His commandments' — and not the commandments of the evil impulse; 'all His statutes' — and not the statutes of the evil impulse. And what are the enticements of the evil impulse? A parable — to what may it be compared? To a king who appointed officials over his kingdom and over his merchandise, each one in charge of a treasury: one in charge of treasuries of good food, and one in charge of treasories of gems. All the world came to buy from the treasuries of good food. This one who was in charge of the treasury of gems saw that no one was buying from him and became jealous. What did he do? He sent his agents to destroy the weak houses, for against the strong ones he could not prevail. He said: Be that as it may, if they make the effort to destroy one of the strong houses, they will destroy twenty weak ones, and all of them will come to buy gems from me, and I will not be lesser than my fellow. And this is what is written (Jeremiah 1:14): ״מצפון תפתח הרעה על כל יושבי הארץ״ וגו׳. Therefore it says: ושמרת כל חקיו — and when you keep all His statutes — כל המחלה אשר שמתי. Why did it say כל? To shut before him all the doors: for when he does not find you soft one time and hard one time — when you keep all His statutes, all the disease which I put upon Egypt through him — I will not put upon you. What is כי אני ה׳ רופאך? And when did He strike him? Rather, when he comes and strikes — I am HaShem your healer — end of quotation in the Sefer ha-Bahir." "<b>And I have heard further</b> that the ten plagues, whose mnemonic-sign is דצ״ך עד״ש באח״ב — this sign sums to five hundred and one, which is the numerical value of אש״ר. And therefore Scripture says: כל המחלה — 'all the disease,' which is the numerical value of אש״ר — 'which I put upon Egypt, I will not put upon you, for I am HaShem who heals you.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {4 "The mnemonic דצ״ך עד״ש באח״ב comprises the initial letters of the ten plagues per R. Yehudah's abbreviation (d.tz.k. = דם צפרדע כינים, etc.). The gematria of these letter-values and the word אש״ר are reproduced exactly as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:10.207580668Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}